BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Symphonic Band &#8211\; Eric M. Smedley\, conductor
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261028T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261028T213000
UID:b462b41625ff1cbc497965ba4443dfa0@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:Featuring graduate student conductor Anthony McDonnell\nEnjoy 
 this performance from almost anywhere in the world via LIVE@jacobs!\nReper
 toire\nJoseph Turrin: Academy Overture (2017)\nBenson: The Leaves are Fall
 ing (1964)\nCorey Chang: What’s Next? (2024/25\, premiere\, D.M. dissert
 ation)\nJoni Greene: AfterGlow (2024)\nJodie Blackshaw: 22° halo (2022)\n
 Alfred Reed: Russian Christmas Music (1944/69)\nAbout the Conductor\nEric 
 M. Smedley is associate professor of music in bands at the Indiana Univers
 ity Jacobs School of Music\, where he directs the Symphonic Band\, teaches
  undergraduate and graduate instrumental conducting courses\, and serves a
 s the director of the Big Red Basketball Band. Smedley earned a Doctor of 
 Musical Arts degree from the University of Washington and Master of Music 
 and Bachelor of Music Education degrees from the Jacobs School of Music. H
 e previously served on the music faculties of Boise State University and W
 estern Kentucky University and as a public-school music educator in Cocoa 
 Beach\, Florida\; Santa Monica\, California\; and Kenosha\, Wisconsin. He 
 has conducted throughout North America and in Vietnam\, Poland\, Russia\, 
 and Japan. Smedley maintains an active schedule as a clinician\, guest con
 ductor\, adjudicator\, and trumpet performer. His Symphonic Band was selec
 ted to perform at the 2018 College Band Directors National Association Nor
 th Central Conference. He was a finalist at the 2023 Warsaw Wind Band Cond
 ucting Competition in Warsaw\, Poland. In 2025\, Navona Records released t
 he album Origins and Expansions\, featuring a composition by Kevin Bobo\, 
 world-renowned steel pan soloist Liam Teague\, and the IU Wind Ensemble. S
 medley’s research interests are varied and include projects in the areas
  of contemporary wind band literature\, wind band orchestration\, and cond
 ucting pedagogy. Smedley is a member of the Gamma chapter of the internati
 onal band fraternity Phi Beta Mu and is an honorary member of Kappa Kappa 
 Psi\, Tau Beta Sigma\, and Phi Mu Alpha Sinfonia. He is a member of the Co
 llege Band Directors National Association and the National Association for
  Music Education. He lives in Bloomington\, Indiana\, with his wife\, Jess
 ica\, their daughter\, Ella\, and their Bedlington Terriers\, Piper and Fi
 nn.
GEO:39.1654576;-86.5183076
LOCATION:Musical Arts Center\, 200 S. Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\, IN 4
 7405
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/symphonic-band-8211-eric-m-smed
 ley-conductor/59661/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Treasures of the Silk Road
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261028T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261028T180000
UID:4af4d6c3c975f0581e7bd3129f266002@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:The McCalla Center\, in collaboration with musician and schola
 r Shahyar Daneshgar\, celebrates the opening of its new exhibition Treasur
 es of the Silk Road on Friday\, September 25th\, 4:00 pm-6:00 pm EDT.\nThe
  exhibit features over 70 musical instruments from the Pastel-Daneshgar mu
 sical instrument collection\, with a focus on strings\, flutes\, and percu
 ssion from across Asia\, the Middle East\, and North Africa. Crafted from 
 wood\, snakeskin\, horsehair\, lambskin\, camel bone\, and other unique ma
 terials\, these instruments provide a compelling history of cultural and a
 rtistic exchanges that have led to the myriad musical sounds and styles th
 at permeate the world today. The opening event will feature remarks from t
 he collection owner\, Shahyar Daneshgar\, and will feature musical clips p
 rovided by the Lotus World Music & Arts Festival.\nShahyar Daneshgar is a 
 lifelong musician\, scholar\, and cultural ambassador whose passion for tr
 aditional music has shaped more than three decades of collecting\, teachin
 g\, and performing. Born into a large musical family\, he studied at the T
 ehran Conservatory of Music before pursuing academic work in Germany and l
 ater at Indiana University\, where he earned his Ph.D. in Central Eurasian
  Studies in 1995. He taught at Indiana University until his retirement in 
 2024. He is founder and director of the Bloomington Silk Road Ensemble.\nT
 he exhibition is free and runs from September 25\, 2026\, to September 202
 7.\nThe mission of the McCalla Center is to provide accessible exhibition 
 and educational experiences\, creating opportunities for students\, facult
 y\, and the community to engage with collections\, IU-generated creative a
 ctivity\, and community projects in meaningful and innovative ways.
GEO:39.1709246;-86.527549
LOCATION:McCalla Center\, McCalla Center\, 525 E. 9th Street\, Bloomington
 \, IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/treasures-of-the-silk-road/5960
 4/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
