BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Treasures of the Silk Road
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261109T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261109T180000
UID:83fe68d4b109830ae55bbd73f8abb4a1@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:The McCalla Center\, in collaboration with musician and schola
 r Shahyar Daneshgar\, celebrates the opening of its new exhibition Treasur
 es of the Silk Road on Friday\, September 25th\, 4:00 pm-6:00 pm EDT.\nThe
  exhibit features over 70 musical instruments from the Pastel-Daneshgar mu
 sical instrument collection\, with a focus on strings\, flutes\, and percu
 ssion from across Asia\, the Middle East\, and North Africa. Crafted from 
 wood\, snakeskin\, horsehair\, lambskin\, camel bone\, and other unique ma
 terials\, these instruments provide a compelling history of cultural and a
 rtistic exchanges that have led to the myriad musical sounds and styles th
 at permeate the world today. The opening event will feature remarks from t
 he collection owner\, Shahyar Daneshgar\, and will feature musical clips p
 rovided by the Lotus World Music & Arts Festival.\nShahyar Daneshgar is a 
 lifelong musician\, scholar\, and cultural ambassador whose passion for tr
 aditional music has shaped more than three decades of collecting\, teachin
 g\, and performing. Born into a large musical family\, he studied at the T
 ehran Conservatory of Music before pursuing academic work in Germany and l
 ater at Indiana University\, where he earned his Ph.D. in Central Eurasian
  Studies in 1995. He taught at Indiana University until his retirement in 
 2024. He is founder and director of the Bloomington Silk Road Ensemble.\nT
 he exhibition is free and runs from September 25\, 2026\, to September 202
 7.\nThe mission of the McCalla Center is to provide accessible exhibition 
 and educational experiences\, creating opportunities for students\, facult
 y\, and the community to engage with collections\, IU-generated creative a
 ctivity\, and community projects in meaningful and innovative ways.
GEO:39.1709246;-86.527549
LOCATION:McCalla Center\, McCalla Center\, 525 E. 9th Street\, Bloomington
 \, IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/treasures-of-the-silk-road/5960
 4/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Vindication Swim w/ Q&A | Additional Films and Guests
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261109T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261109T211500
UID:20810c13b46835dbe410f272d9f6008b@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:Vindication Swim tells the remarkable story of Mercedes Gleitz
 e\, who in 1927 became the first British woman to swim the English Channel
 . Facing press hostility\, anti-immigrant bias\, and constraints on women 
 in sport\, Mercedes’ achievement remains a powerful testament to her gri
 t\, courage\, and determination. Writer-director Elliott Hasler—only 23 
 when the film premiered—pursued period authenticity through grassroots c
 ollaboration with coastal communities throughout the UK. Three years in th
 e making\, the film’s swimming sequences were shot miles out in the Chan
 nel\, vividly capturing the perils of Mercedes’ epic crossing. [98 min\;
  drama\; English]\nA Q&A with director Elliott Hasler and Radhika Paramesw
 aran (Herman B Wells Endowed Professor\, Media School) will follow the scr
 eening.
GEO:39.168585;-86.517361
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 E. 7th Street\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/vindication-swim-w-qa-additiona
 l-films-and-guests/59997/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:What's Happening to Climate Journalism?
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261109T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261109T153000
UID:d8178e0d328be5f2d66ad5617a7b38ef@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:2026 has already proven to be one of the hottest years on reco
 rd\, but it has also seen a decline in news coverage of climate change nat
 ionwide. This panel will examine the dynamics that influence news attentio
 n to climate change and consider the challenges of reporting the beat in a
  contested political and economic environment for climate journalism.\nPan
 elists: \n• Ryan Comfort\, The Media School \n• Suzannah Evans Comfort
 \, The Media School \n• Karl Schneider\, IndyStar
GEO:39.1670172;-86.5262462
LOCATION:Franklin Hall\, Room: Commons\, 601 E Kirkwood Ave.\,  Bloomingto
 n\, IN 47405
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/whats-happening-to-climate-jour
 nalism/60116/
