BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Straight No Chaser
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261210T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261210T203000
UID:6d9f21bfb150d476b35dba5681c52b8f@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:The holiday season is not complete without the return of a cap
 pella sensations Straight No Chaser—born right here at Indiana Universit
 y. The group has become synonymous with the sound of the holidays\, delive
 ring crowd-pleasing arrangements with humor\, heart\, and flawless harmoni
 es. This year’s performance will be part of their nationwide Making Spir
 its Bright Tour.\nThe nine-man act\, including Walter Chase\, Jerome Colli
 ns\, Seggie Isho\, Michael Luginbill\, Steve Morgan\, Luke Bob Robinson\, 
 Jasper Smith\, Tyler Trepp\, and Freedom Young\, has served as a festive b
 lend of harmonious voices for three decades.\nThis year’s tour is a gift
  that requires no wrapping and one that everyone delights in sharing. To d
 ate\, the band has sold more than three million albums worldwide\, sold mo
 re than 1.5 million tickets worldwide\, earned 100 million+ YouTube views
 \, and racked up more than a billion streams.
GEO:39.1684021;-86.5178918
LOCATION:IU Auditorium\, 1211 E. 7th Street\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/straight-no-chaser/59293/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Treasures of the Silk Road
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261210T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261210T180000
UID:fa2ae8f08bf60033c59300edff4901ce@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:The McCalla Center\, in collaboration with musician and schola
 r Shahyar Daneshgar\, celebrates the opening of its new exhibition Treasur
 es of the Silk Road on Friday\, September 25th\, 4:00 pm-6:00 pm EDT.\nThe
  exhibit features over 70 musical instruments from the Pastel-Daneshgar mu
 sical instrument collection\, with a focus on strings\, flutes\, and percu
 ssion from across Asia\, the Middle East\, and North Africa. Crafted from 
 wood\, snakeskin\, horsehair\, lambskin\, camel bone\, and other unique ma
 terials\, these instruments provide a compelling history of cultural and a
 rtistic exchanges that have led to the myriad musical sounds and styles th
 at permeate the world today. The opening event will feature remarks from t
 he collection owner\, Shahyar Daneshgar\, and will feature musical clips p
 rovided by the Lotus World Music & Arts Festival.\nShahyar Daneshgar is a 
 lifelong musician\, scholar\, and cultural ambassador whose passion for tr
 aditional music has shaped more than three decades of collecting\, teachin
 g\, and performing. Born into a large musical family\, he studied at the T
 ehran Conservatory of Music before pursuing academic work in Germany and l
 ater at Indiana University\, where he earned his Ph.D. in Central Eurasian
  Studies in 1995. He taught at Indiana University until his retirement in 
 2024. He is founder and director of the Bloomington Silk Road Ensemble.\nT
 he exhibition is free and runs from September 25\, 2026\, to September 202
 7.\nThe mission of the McCalla Center is to provide accessible exhibition 
 and educational experiences\, creating opportunities for students\, facult
 y\, and the community to engage with collections\, IU-generated creative a
 ctivity\, and community projects in meaningful and innovative ways.
GEO:39.1709246;-86.527549
LOCATION:McCalla Center\, McCalla Center\, 525 E. 9th Street\, Bloomington
 \, IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/treasures-of-the-silk-road/5960
 4/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Annie
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261210
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270104
UID:af3a57fc368015ec504a878495906402@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:Constellation Stage & Screen presents “Annie” on stage Dec
 ember 10th - January 3rd at the Waldron Auditorium.\nDiscover the magic of
  Annie\, the iconic Broadway musical that has captured hearts for generati
 ons! The timeless tale of Little Orphan Annie follows a tenacious young gi
 rl whose unbreakable optimism leads her on an unforgettable journey from a
  New York City orphanage to a place she can finally call home. Featuring o
 ne of Broadway’s most memorable scores\, including “It’s the Hard-Kn
 ock Life\,” “Easy Street\,” “N.Y.C.” and the endlessly-uplifting
  “Tomorrow\,” Annie is a delightful theatrical experience for the enti
 re family. Fun for all ages!\nSee Constellation’s website for more infor
 mation\, including a detailed performance schedule. Learn more and find ti
 ckets at https://seeconstellation.org/kids/annie/
GEO:39.1662918;-86.5338119
LOCATION:Waldron Arts Center\, 112 S Walnut St\, Bloomington\, IN 47403
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/annie/58936/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Samuel Levi Jones: An Archaeology of Knowledge
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261210
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261211
UID:00f18ae942e86ab31790fd77a01f4512@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:For more than a decade\, Indiana-born Samuel Levi Jones has cr
 eated abstract assemblages with found printed and bound matter\, deconstru
 cting encyclopedias\, law books\, medical texts\, and other didactic mater
 ials. Jones removes their fabric covers and pulps their pages\, sewing the
 se swatches together and applying paper pulp to create texturally rich\, c
 ollaged works with raw edges\, loose threads\, and worn surfaces. His proc
 ess of physically destroying archival materials and reconstructing them in
 to artworks constitutes a materially engaged critique of institutional aut
 hority\, reflecting on systems of knowledge that uphold power and privileg
 e in the United States. For his exhibition at the Eskenazi Museum\, Jones 
 mined the museum’s collection for works that resonate with the themes he
  interrogates\, including nationalism and patriotism\, censorship\, and th
 e representation marginalized identities in historical narratives. Borrowi
 ng its title from the ideas of the influential French philosopher Michel F
 oucault\, Samuel Levi Jones: An Archaeology of Knowledge comprises ten yea
 rs of Jones’ investigations into the underlying structures that shape ho
 w we see\, speak\, and think about culture\, politics\, and power.\nSuppor
 t for this exhibition has been generously provided by the Robert and Sara 
 LeBien Director's Endowment\, Charlie and Jeanetta Nelms\, and Janice L. W
 iggins.
GEO:39.1688819;-86.5198163
LOCATION:Eskenazi Museum of Art at Indiana University\, 1133 E. 7th Street
 \, Fine Arts Plaza\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/samuel-levi-jones-an-archaeolog
 y-of-knowledge/60099/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Photographic Reflex: The Lee Marks and John C. DePrez Jr. Coll
 ection
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261210
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261211
UID:21e9823d254e46f081aaa12dfeccec75@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:Spanning more than 150 years\, The Photographic Reflex celebra
 tes a transformative gift to the Eskenazi Museum of Art at Indiana Univers
 ity: more than one hundred works by eighty artists\, generously donated by
  Lee Marks and John C. DePrez Jr. This exhibition features photographs dat
 ing from 1856 to 2017 and highlights the medium’s extraordinary range\, 
 both as a tool for documentation and as a vehicle for artistic exploration
 .\nFrom Alfred Stieglitz’s delicate studies of grass to Richard Misrach
 ’s vast images of the cosmos\, photographers have long found inspiration
  in the natural world. The built environment has also served as a compelli
 ng subject\, and the exhibition includes Berenice Abbott’s striking view
 s of 1920s New York City as well as Edward Burtynsky’s sweeping industri
 al landscapes.\nPhotography has played a central role in witnessing confli
 ct\, from Timothy H. O’Sullivan’s Civil War battlefield to iconic imag
 es from World War II\, Vietnam\, and beyond. While often associated with s
 traightforward depictions of reality\, photography also allows artists to 
 experiment with light\, exposure\, and process to construct imaginative sc
 enes and images beyond human sight.\nThe Photographic Reflex: The Lee Mark
 s and John C. DePrez Jr. Collection reveals photography’s power to refle
 ct and shape our perception of the world around us across time\, space\, a
 nd vision.\nLead support for this exhibition has been generously provided 
 by the Martha and David Moore Prints\, Drawings and Photographs Endowment
 \; Peter and Pamela DePrez\; Anne Marks Morgan\; Gregory Morse\; Efroymson
  Family Fund\; Anne N. DePrez\; John J. Medveckis\; and Friends of Lee Mar
 ks and John DePrez Jr.\nAdditional support provided by John M. (Jack) Reyn
 olds\, Jeffrey A. Wolin\, Sandi Clark\, and Katherine Campbell and Robert 
 J. Boeglin Jr.\, M.D.
GEO:39.1688819;-86.5198163
LOCATION:Eskenazi Museum of Art at Indiana University\, 1133 E. 7th Street
 \, Fine Arts Plaza\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/the-photographic-reflex-the-lee
 -marks-and-john-c-deprez-jr-collection/60101/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
